Z||000032||Formula88||07-05-2001||10:28 AM||||Well, Amrerican car manufacturers COULD build technically innovative and advanced designs, but they choose not to.  The GN's engine was an evolutionary wonder.  No telling where development could have put it by today.  How about the 3.4TDC?  After 10 years of development and some factory turbocharging, that could be one of the best modern engines in the world.  American manufacturers tend to CHOSE not to innovate because it's cheaper.  The 3800SC is a simpler solution than the high tech, high maintenance 3.4TDC.  Pushrod engines are cheaper to design, build, and WARRANTY, than the high-tech engines.  Our economy is strong enough and gas prices are low enough not to make these innovations necessary.  If we were paying $4/gal of gas, we'd be screaming for GM to build smaller, cheaper, better cars that get good mileage and still perform well.  Just my $.02.  <P>Oh, and I believe the GN was the fastest production American car in 87, not in the world.  Also, the definition of "production" typically excludes limited run specialty vehicles that may have been faster.  I loved the power of my GN, but it was at it's heart, a Buick Regal.  So it was fast in a straight line, but handling left a lot to be desired and it just didn't have the right "feel" to me.  That's why I love my Pontiacs.  They feel right.  They have the right attitude for me.||||reg||1
Z||000033||TRiAD||07-05-2001||12:15 PM||||Fact of the matter is the GN<B>X</B>, NOT the GN, beat the Vette to 60mph, but NOT in the 1/4 mile. They were the 2nd quickest production car to 60. <BR>They did NOT corner worth crap. Neither did the regular GNs. <P>There were many cars in their day that would destroy them on a road course. The ONLY thing they were good at was accelleration, and only 0-60 at that. <P>I'd like to see the "300mph" car noted in your beloved ad. <BR>The HP and torque were; Peak Power: 245 hp @ 4400 RPM SAE net <BR>Peak Torque: 355 lbf·ft. @ 2000 RPM SAE net <BR>Not bad, but nothing exceptional. <P>You know what this "magical" 0-60 was? I'll tell you. About 6.0 seconds. About 14.6 sec 1/4 mile. <BR>That Skyline? 0-60 in 5.2, 1/4 mile in 13.7. And that's in US Legalized trim, with much less power than on it's home shores. The Honda S2000 (mentioned earlier) runs 0-60 in 5.5 and the 1/4 in 14.1. <P>Sorry, but your "dinosaur" (your term) was decent in it's day, but it was only good at one thing, and many MANY cars outdo it at it's own game today. <P>Sorry. <P>Here's a Turbo Regal Webring, for your enjoyment...   <A HREF="http://nav.webring.yahoo.com/hub?ring=gnttype&id=161&hub" TARGET=_blank>http://nav.webring.yahoo.com/hub?ring=gnttype&id=161&hub</A> <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Slammed Fiero||07-05-2001||12:27 PM||||THANK YOU TRIAD<P>Finally!!!<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000035||KnightRyder31||07-05-2001||12:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Fact of the matter is the GN[b]X</B>, NOT the GN, beat the Vette to 60mph, but NOT in the 1/4 mile. They were the 2nd quickest production car to 60. <BR>They did NOT corner worth crap. Neither did the regular GNs. <P>There were many cars in their day that would destroy them on a road course. The ONLY thing they were good at was accelleration, and only 0-60 at that. <P>I'd like to see the "300mph" car noted in your beloved ad. <BR>The HP and torque were; Peak Power: 245 hp @ 4400 RPM SAE net <BR>Peak Torque: 355 lbf·ft. @ 2000 RPM SAE net <BR>Not bad, but nothing exceptional. <P>You know what this "magical" 0-60 was? I'll tell you. About 6.0 seconds. About 14.6 sec 1/4 mile. <BR>That Skyline? 0-60 in 5.2, 1/4 mile in 13.7. And that's in US Legalized trim, with much less power than on it's home shores. The Honda S2000 (mentioned earlier) runs 0-60 in 5.5 and the 1/4 in 14.1. <P>Sorry, but your "dinosaur" (your term) was decent in it's day, but it was only good at one thing, and many MANY cars outdo it at it's own game today. <P>Sorry. <P>Here's a Turbo Regal Webring, for your enjoyment...   <A HREF="http://nav.webring.yahoo.com/hub?ring=gnttype&id=161&hub" TARGET=_blank>http://nav.webring.yahoo.com/hub?ring=gnttype&id=161&hub</A> <P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>1986 Grand National times- <BR>0-60=4.9           1/4 mile=13.9<P>1986 Chevrolet Corvette times-                    0-60=5.8            1/4 mile=14.4<P>Have a nice day   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by KnightRyder31 (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||Slammed Fiero||07-05-2001||12:43 PM||||Where did you find those #'s???<P>The TA with the 3.8 Turbo could only muster a 5.4 to sixty , all the GN's I have ever seen run mid 14's , and from my own personal experience with an 87 vette 14.11 in the 1/4 and 5.4 to sixty.<P>the GNX on the other hand produced numbers a lot closer to what you are quoting for the GN<P>JM ||||reg||1
