Z||000039||Formula88||07-05-2001||12:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Fact of the matter is the GN[b]X</B>, NOT the GN, beat the Vette to 60mph, but NOT in the 1/4 mile. They were the 2nd quickest production car to 60. <BR>They did NOT corner worth crap. Neither did the regular GNs. <BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Acutally, it was the regular GN that beat the 87 Vette, and in the quarter mile, too.  Stock GNs were running low 14s to high 13s with cool air.  But you're right, they don't corner for crap.  They corner like a Buick Regal.  But their forte was definately 1/4 mile instead of 0-60.  With turbo lag or overboost making a perfect launch difficult, they really came alive in 2nd gear and started to charge hard all the way through the quarter mile.  The intercooler really didn't help much till you got some speed up, then it made a difference.<P>Not trying to start a flame war here.  Just speaking from personal experiance.  I had an '87 Grand National that I bought new back in '87.  I know what they could do.  I'm not relying on a magazine article.<P>Heat was the big determining factor.  I clocked my GN from a 5mph roll to 60 in 3.5 seconds once.  Now before you call BS, there are some disclaimers.  Yes, it was from a roll, because the roads were too cold for any kind of traction.  And the ambient temp was about -9F.  So, not normal circumstances, but it illustrates the potential.  I read in one test that for every degree F you drop the intake air temp on the GN, you got 1 HP.  That seemed about right given how much a difference I could feel between a cold and hot day.  ||||reg||1
Z||000040||KnightRyder31||07-05-2001||12:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> Acutally, it was the regular GN that beat the 87 Vette, and in the quarter mile, too.  Stock GNs were running low 14s to high 13s with cool air.  But you're right, they don't corner for crap.  They corner like a Buick Regal.  But their forte was definately 1/4 mile instead of 0-60.  With turbo lag or overboost making a perfect launch difficult, they really came alive in 2nd gear and started to charge hard all the way through the quarter mile.  The intercooler really didn't help much till you got some speed up, then it made a difference.<P>Not trying to start a flame war here.  Just speaking from personal experiance.  I had an '87 Grand National that I bought new back in '87.  I know what they could do.  I'm not relying on a magazine article.<P>Heat was the big determining factor.  I clocked my GN from a 5mph roll to 60 in 3.5 seconds once.  Now before you call BS, there are some disclaimers.  Yes, it was from a roll, because the roads were too cold for any kind of traction.  And the ambient temp was about -9F.  So, not normal circumstances, but it illustrates the potential.  I read in one test that for every degree F you drop the intake air temp on the GN, you got 1 HP.  That seemed about right given how much a difference I could feel between a cold and hot day.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thank you Formula88.<BR>I agree 100%.<BR>||||reg||1
