Z||000051||saleenfiero||07-05-2001||06:30 PM||||The grand national engine was great.  Don't get me wrong, but it is really nothing more than a good tuned engine with a turbo.  And it is great that it was the fastest car to be had for under 100k 14 yrs ago, but when you start comparing it to the engines of today, it looks pitiful.  BMW and Porsche have the best developed 6 cylinder engines in the world and perfectly balanced.  They have power when you want it and revs to match.  If the GN is the best 1987 had, then why compare it to the best 2001 has?  Fossils can't win.||||reg||1
Z||000052||hugh||07-05-2001||08:54 PM||||This is my experience with the Grand National.I've owned mine since about 1991.When I got it,it was without a doubt the fastest car I had ever driven.I kept making changes,and had to keep increasing my rear tire width.I used to go to Atco dragway,which is less than 10 miles from my house,on Fridays.The best run I made was a 12.20@115mph.I was running 10x26 inch slicks,a dump tube,stock 3.43 rear,shift kit in the trans,some head work,adjustable fuel pressure regulator,Bosch fuel pump,basically a stock turbo,larger intercooler,and new spark plugs.My 60 foot time was 1.95(terrible)because I just blew the tires off through 1st and 2nd.My 1/8th was 7.95@just under 90 mph.The poor traction was because of weak front springs letting the front ride too low and losing weight transfer.All this was with a stock block 231 cubic inch engine with about 30,000 miles on it.Shipping weight on this car is(I believe)3520 lbs. ||||reg||1
