Z||000076||Will||07-08-2001||09:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sleeper:<BR><B> it just flew right over your head...listen to JohnnyK, there IS replacement for displacement. my friend has a 66 nova with built 383 and my other friend has a 90 vw corrado 1.6L with a Garett T-3 turbo. theres like what, over 4 liter differance. they race all the time and the vw wins every time, not by much but it still a win and proves JohnnyK point.<P><BR>something else for import haters to look upon:<P><BR>2.2L's of vtec ALL MOTOR low 10 second excitement. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It did not fly right over my head.<BR>I just think that you're comparing apples to oranges when you compare a high cammed, high boosted small engine to a low cammed, NA large engine.<BR>If you compare a high cammed, high boosted large engine to a high cammed high boosted small engine, which one will make more torque and power? The large engine, every time.<BR>Comparing apples to apples, how would your friend's turbo VW do against Lingengelter's turbo Corvette or turbo Viper? Not too well, I'd imagine.<BR>What do those cars have which your friend's VW does not? Displacement. The Viper and Vette aren't even running much boost, comparatively; less than 10 psi. They're even a lot heavier than your friend's Corrado, too.<P>If there's no replacement for displacement, why do NA Honda drag engines, like the one in the car in your picture, displace far more than their production counterparts?<BR>Look in this month's Sport Compact Car: they describe 2.4 litre engines built out of B series blocks, and 2.6 litre engines built from H series blocks.<BR>If there were no replacement for displacement, why would those engines be any bigger than production engines?<P>Speed in the 1/4 is related to a lot more things than just power. I don't know it's 1/4 mile time, but the Opel speedster runs 5.5 0-60 with 150 hp. Why? It's light.<BR>How much does your friend's VW weigh? Significantly less than 3000 lbs, even with a full interior, right?<BR>How much does your other friend's 66 Nova weigh? I'm betting it has a full interior and weighs in around 3600 lbs.<P>That Honda may break into the ten's, but it sure as heck won't do it on those tires, and it won't be a street drivable machine, either.||||reg||1
Z||000077||hugh||07-08-2001||10:42 AM||||My GN is not factory stock,but when you consider the cost of those other cars I'm sure I still have spent less money.Speed on the drag strip is a product of horsepower.I have gone 115MPH in the 1/4.That computes to anywhere from 428 to 441 HP for the weight of my car.This is with a 231 cubic inch engine(3.8liter).That works out to over 100 hp per liter. ||||reg||1
Z||000078||TRiAD||07-08-2001||11:13 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>...for those who haven't noticed,the Grand National is a 5 passenger car(as opposed to two passenger).It may be 14 years old,but does anyone have pictures of another car that can carry 5 people in relative comfort on a long drive,get descent mileage,and have as good straight line acceleration...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was answering a question. He never said "a cheap 14 year old car" that could do it. <P>And Black, read the article you posted. It says who made the engine. Since when is Cosworth or SVI domestic?<P>...And I quote...<BR><I>"...check out the twin-turbo 16-valve DOHC <B>Cosworth</B> cross-flow 2.2 <B>developed by SVI</B> under contract for PPG."</I><P>For someone who has said MANY times "we all have opinions, let's not fight over them" you sure flame like a 7th grader.<P><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
